---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Hero from "../components/Hero.astro";
import OfferCard from "../components/OfferCard.astro";
---
<BaseLayout
	title="Work with me — Lead From The Hip"
	description="Two engagements, both bought by the leader and both ending in a ninety-day behavioral re-measure. Private 360 and full assessment-to-route coaching."
>
	<Hero
		eyebrow="Engagements"
		standfirst="Everything here is bought by the leader, not by their company. You choose the timing, you own the data, and nothing produced in an engagement enters anyone's personnel file."
	>
		Two ways in. <span class="accent">Both end with evidence.</span>
	</Hero>

	<section class="max-w-[1080px] mx-auto px-6 py-12 border-t border-rule">
		<div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
			<OfferCard
				id="private-360"
				price="[PRICE — brief suggests $450–750]"
				duration="About three weeks end to end"
				includes="Rater protocol · administration · themed report · 45-minute readout"
				title="The Private 360"
				description="Almost every 360 you have ever been given was commissioned by your employer. They chose the instrument, the timing, and often the raters. The results went into a system you don't control, at a moment that suited someone else's calendar. There is no reason it has to work that way. A 360 is only a corporate instrument because companies were the ones with the budget. This one is yours. You commission it, you nominate the raters, you pick the moment. I administer it, aggregate the responses, and theme the comments. You never see raw responses and neither does anyone else — a four-rater minimum per group protects anonymity, and I tell your raters exactly that when I invite them."
				bullets={[
					"A structured behavioral instrument, not a personality test",
					"Four to six raters you nominate, across the groups you care about",
					"Themed verbatim comments — never quoted in a way that identifies a rater",
					"A 45-minute readout focused on what to do, not on what the numbers mean",
					"A file that exists in exactly one place: with you",
				]}
				whoFor="Anyone who suspects the people around them are being kind rather than accurate. If you are hard to rattle and pleasant to work for, you are probably getting less honest feedback than you think."
				ctaLabel="Book a call →"
				ctaHref="[BOOKING URL]"
			/>
			<OfferCard
				id="terrain-route"
				price="[PRICE — brief suggests $1,800–3,000]"
				duration="Ninety days"
				includes="Assessment battery · 90-minute debrief · written route · 90-day re-measure"
				title="Terrain & Route"
				subtitle="The full arc — terrain, then a route built around it."
				description="The full arc, and the reason the practice exists. We start by mapping your terrain — the dispositions that make some leadership behaviors cheap for you and others permanently expensive. Then we find the two or three beliefs that fire when you are under load, because those are what actually drive your behavior on a bad Thursday, not your intentions on a good Monday. Then you get a route: two or three reps, each with a cue, a slot, and a script, designed around your terrain rather than in spite of it. Not more discipline. Better scaffolding. Ninety days later we measure. Not the personality — that hasn't moved and it isn't supposed to. The behavior, rated by the people who work for you, compared to where you started."
				bullets={[
					'A validated assessment battery, professionally interpreted <span class="placeholder">(Certifications: [DAVID — list your certifications here])</span>',
					"A 90-minute debrief that spends most of its time on what to do",
					"A written route: two or three prescribed reps, with cues and scripts",
					"A ninety-day behavioral re-measure, included — not sold separately",
					"A short written comparison of where you started and where you landed",
				]}
				whoFor="Leaders who have done the assessment thing before, found it accurate, and watched it change nothing."
				ctaLabel="Book a call →"
				ctaHref="[BOOKING URL]"
			/>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="max-w-[1080px] mx-auto px-6 py-12 border-t border-rule">
		<h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl mb-2">What I won't do</h2>
		<p class="text-muted measure mb-6">Worth saying plainly, since a lot of this field doesn't.</p>
		<ul class="flex flex-col gap-4 text-ink measure">
			<li>
				<strong>I won't sell you a type.</strong> You are not a color, a letter, or an
				animal. The assessment is a starting point for designing behavior, not an
				identity.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>I won't promise personality change.</strong> Dispositions are stable
				across adulthood. Anyone telling you otherwise is selling something. Behavior is
				trainable; that is what we work on.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>I won't provide therapy.</strong> This is behavioral coaching that
				borrows a cognitive framework. If something clinical surfaces I will say so and
				refer you on.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>I won't report on you to anyone.</strong> Not to your manager, not to
				your HR team, not in aggregate. If your employer is paying, we agree the
				boundary in writing first.
			</li>
		</ul>
	</section>

	<section class="max-w-[1080px] mx-auto px-6 py-12 border-t border-rule">
		<h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl mb-6">FAQ</h2>
		<div class="flex flex-col gap-6 measure">
			<div>
				<h3 class="text-lg mb-1">How much of my time does this take?</h3>
				<p class="text-ink text-sm">
					The assessment is about an hour. The debrief is ninety minutes. After that
					the reps themselves are designed to cost under five minutes a week — if a
					rep needs a recurring block on your calendar, it has been designed wrong.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div>
				<h3 class="text-lg mb-1">Does my employer find out?</h3>
				<p class="text-ink text-sm">
					Not from me. Everything is commissioned by you and delivered to you. If your
					company is paying, we agree in writing beforehand exactly what they receive
					— usually confirmation that the engagement happened, and nothing else.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div>
				<h3 class="text-lg mb-1">What if the ninety-day measure shows nothing changed?</h3>
				<p class="text-ink text-sm">
					Then it shows nothing changed, and you find out from data instead of from a
					feeling. That is the point of including it.{" "}
					<span class="placeholder">
						[DAVID — decide and state your policy here: a second cycle at reduced
						cost, a diagnostic session, or nothing. Pick one and commit.]
					</span>
				</p>
			</div>
			<div>
				<h3 class="text-lg mb-1">Which assessments do you use?</h3>
				<p class="placeholder border border-dashed border-flag bg-flag-soft rounded-[3px] p-4 text-sm">
					[DAVID — name your certifications here in plain terms. Do not reproduce
					publisher scale names or interpretive content; see
					docs/08-licensing-guardrails.md.]
				</p>
			</div>
			<div>
				<h3 class="text-lg mb-1">Do you work with teams?</h3>
				<p class="text-ink text-sm">Occasionally, and it is a different engagement. Ask.</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>
</BaseLayout>
